    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In a parliamentary democracy voters elect a parliament, and the government is formed from it and stays in office only as long as it keeps the confidence of a parliamentary majority.</li><li>The system grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, where between the 1720s and the 1830s real executive power passed from the monarch to a prime minister and cabinet answerable to the House of Commons.</li><li>Spread through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> after 1918 and 1945, it is praised for flexibility and clear responsibility and blamed for unstable coalitions and weak checks on a majority.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>’s Basic Law lets the Bundestag remove a chancellor only by electing a successor in the same vote, a rule meant to avoid the crises of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>; it has succeeded once, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-kohl">Helmut Kohl</a> replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-schmidt">Helmut Schmidt</a> in 1982.</p></aside>
    </section>

