    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1721</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-walpole">Robert Walpole</a> heads the British government until 1742; he is usually counted the first prime minister</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1782</span><span class="rf-text">Lord North resigns in the face of the first motion of no confidence in a whole British government</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1832</span><span class="rf-text">The Reform Act widens the franchise and strengthens the Commons’ hold on the choice of government</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> and the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> win responsible government, the first British colonies to do so</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1911</span><span class="rf-text">The Parliament Act ends the House of Lords’ absolute veto over bills passed by the House of Commons</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1933</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> passes the Enabling Act, ending parliamentary government in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1949</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>’s Basic Law introduces the constructive vote of no confidence</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1950</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>’s constitution makes it a parliamentary republic on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster model</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="works">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/westminster-system.jpg" alt="Westminster system" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster system</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Houses.of.parliament.overall.arp.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adrian Pingstone, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In a parliamentary democracy the executive is not separately elected but drawn from, and dependent on, the legislature. Voters elect a parliament, and the head of government, usually called prime minister, premier or chancellor, is the person who can command the support, or “confidence”, of a majority in it, normally the leader of the largest party or of a coalition. The head of state is a separate and largely ceremonial figure: a hereditary monarch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchies</a> such as the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, a president in parliamentary republics such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and Ireland, chosen by parliament, by a special assembly or by popular vote. The head of state usually appoints the government and dissolves parliament in form, but by convention acts on the advice of ministers, keeping only reserve powers for a crisis.</p><p>The government’s life depends on that confidence. If the lower house passes a motion of no confidence or rejects the budget, the government must resign or ask for new elections. Ministers are usually members of parliament and bound by collective responsibility: they settle policy in cabinet and defend it in public, or resign. Walter Bagehot, in The English Constitution of 1867, called this close union, “the nearly complete fusion, of the executive and legislative powers”, the “efficient secret” of the British system, and contrasted the “dignified” parts of the constitution, such as the Crown, with the “efficient” cabinet that actually governs. An official opposition, often with a shadow cabinet of its own, questions the government and offers itself as the alternative.</p><p>Parliamentary systems differ widely. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster model</a>, used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and much of the Commonwealth, elections in single-member districts often give one party a majority, debate is adversarial and the government controls the parliamentary agenda. In the consensus systems of much of continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, as the political scientist Arend Lijphart describes them, proportional representation produces coalitions, committees matter more and parliaments set their own agendas; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and Sweden even require ministers to give up their seats. Germany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> allow a government to be removed only by a constructive vote of no confidence, which elects a successor at the same time. The form differs from presidential democracy, in which a separately elected president serves a fixed term, and from semi-presidential systems such as the French Fifth Republic, in which an elected president shares power with a prime minister answerable to parliament.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon.jpg" alt="Alfonso IX of León" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adeffonsus,_king_of_Galicia_and_Leon_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Parliaments are older than parliamentary government. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/althing">Althing</a> of Iceland met from about 930, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a> summoned townsmen to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes">Cortes</a> in 1188, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> Simon de Montfort’s parliament of 1265 included representatives of the towns; the word comes from the French parler, “to speak”. For centuries such assemblies granted taxes, presented grievances and passed laws, but kings chose their ministers. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> the States General took over the powers of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1581, an early case of an assembly governing in a monarch’s place. In England the civil wars of the 1640s and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1688 settled that the Crown could not rule without Parliament: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bill-of-rights-1689">Bill of Rights 1689</a> forbade taxation and a standing army in peacetime without its consent and protected free speech in its debates, and because money and military discipline were voted for limited periods, Parliament had to meet every year.</p><p>What made the system parliamentary was the passage of executive power from the monarch to ministers who needed the support of the House of Commons. After the Hanoverian succession of 1714 the leading minister, rather than the king, came to chair the cabinet, a change a traditional account explains by George I’s poor English. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-walpole">Robert Walpole</a>, First Lord of the Treasury and Chancellor of the Exchequer from 1721, managed the Commons through patronage and debate for twenty-one years and is usually counted the first prime minister, though the title was then a term of abuse; in 1735 he moved into 10 Downing Street, which he accepted only as the residence of the First Lord. He resigned in 1742 after losing votes in the Commons, and in March 1782, after the defeat at Yorktown, Lord North resigned in the face of a motion that the Commons could “no longer repose confidence in the present ministers”, the first such motion against a whole government.</p><p>Until the 1830s, however, the king still chose ministers and could sustain them, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> sustained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger">William Pitt the Younger</a> against a hostile Commons majority in 1783–1784. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reform-act-1832">Reform Act 1832</a> widened the franchise and weakened royal and aristocratic control of seats, and by the mid-19th century, after Robert Peel had failed to govern without a majority in 1834–1835, it was settled that a government defeated in the Commons must resign or call an election. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a> had defended party in 1770 as a body of men united on a shared principle, and Bagehot gave the mature system its classic description.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="history">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Through History</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-japan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitution-of-japan.jpg" alt="Constitution of Japan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-japan">Constitution of Japan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nihon_Kenpo01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Japanese government, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The British model spread first through the Empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> and the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> won responsible government, a ministry answerable to the elected assembly, in 1848; the Australian colonies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> followed in the 1850s, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of Canada from 1867, the Commonwealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> from 1901, the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and the Irish Free State all governed themselves on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster model</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the franchise widened again in 1867 and 1884, the Parliament Act 1911 ended the House of Lords’ absolute veto, and votes for all men, and for women over thirty who met a property qualification, came in 1918, for all women in 1928.</p><p>Continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> moved more slowly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>’s constitution of 1831 made ministers responsible to parliament, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> followed in 1848, and parliamentarism was established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1884, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1901 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1917, as kings ceased to appoint ministers against the chambers’ will. In the French Third Republic, after President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-de-macmahon">Patrice de MacMahon</a> failed to impose a government on the Chamber of Deputies in 1877, ministries depended wholly on shifting majorities. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> parliamentary republics were founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, but many fell between the wars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> was appointed prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1922 and had made himself dictator by 1926; in Germany, after chancellors had governed for three years by presidential decree, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> passed the Enabling Act in March 1933, letting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>’s government make laws without it.</p><p>After 1945 parliamentary democracy was rebuilt under Allied occupation in Italy, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>, whose Basic Law of 1949 introduced the constructive vote of no confidence, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-japan">constitution of 1947</a> made the cabinet answerable to the Diet. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a republic from 1950, became the largest parliamentary democracy in the world, while in many other former colonies Westminster constitutions gave way to one-party rule or military coups. The French Fourth Republic, with more than twenty governments in twelve years, collapsed in 1958, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>’s Fifth Republic gave much executive power to the president. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> returned to democracy in the 1970s with governments answerable to parliament, and after 1989 so did most of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="strengths">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Strengths</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winston-churchill.jpg" alt="Winston Churchill" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Winston_Churchill_-_19086236948_(restored).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Its defenders have praised above all its flexibility. Walter Bagehot argued that a parliament could change a failing government at any moment without waiting for an election, and a government could call an election when it needed a fresh mandate, while a president elected for a fixed term had to be endured even after losing the country’s trust; he also valued the serious debate that a government facing a watchful opposition must sustain. The fall of Neville Chamberlain in May 1940 is the classic example: after his majority fell from over 200 to 81 in the vote that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> Debate, he resigned within two days, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> formed a coalition of all the main parties, in the middle of a war and without an election.</p><p>A second argument concerns responsibility. Because the government and its parliamentary majority stand or fall together, voters know whom to hold to account; the former US Treasury Secretary C. Douglas Dillon complained that in his own country “the president blames Congress, the Congress blames the president”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a>, decades before he became president, admired British cabinet government in his Congressional Government of 1885. The political scientist R. Kent Weaver notes that a government with a stable majority can pass its programme quickly and adapt policy fast, and a 2001 World Bank study found parliamentary systems associated with less corruption.</p><p>Comparative research has also linked the form with the survival of democracy. Juan Linz argued in 1990 that parliamentary systems, which can replace a leader without a constitutional crisis, are less likely to break down than presidential ones, and Alfred Stepan and Cindy Skach found that new democracies with parliamentary constitutions survived far more often than presidential ones. Arend Lijphart’s comparison of thirty-six democracies concluded that the consensus type, with proportional representation and coalition cabinets, governs at least as effectively as the majoritarian Westminster type and does better on the representation of women and minorities and on social welfare. The Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, whose constructive vote of no confidence has removed a chancellor only once, has not seen the rapid fall of governments that marked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="weaknesses">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Weaknesses</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg.jpg" alt="Paul von Hindenburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-C06886,_Paul_v._Hindenburg_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author., CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The commonest charge is instability where no party commands a majority. Motions of no confidence are rare in two-party systems but far more common in fragmented parliaments, and the French Third and Fourth Republics, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> between the 1950s and the 1990s saw governments fall every year or so. Giovanni Sartori criticised such “assembly government”, in which cabinets are at the mercy of shifting parliamentary majorities. In the Weimar Republic chancellors were repeatedly voted out by majorities that could agree on no successor, until President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> governed through chancellors ruling by emergency decree, and in 1933 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> voted away its own powers. The parliamentary road to office also served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>, whom King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> appointed prime minister in 1922.</p><p>The opposite charge is that a party with a secure majority is too strong. Because the government controls the legislature, few institutions can check it, especially where, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, there is no codified constitution; in 1976 the Conservative politician Lord Hailsham called the result an “elective dictatorship”. Alternating majorities can reverse each other’s laws: the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industry was nationalised in 1951, returned to private owners after 1953 and nationalised again in 1967. R. Kent Weaver notes that party discipline can force members to vote against their constituents’ interests, and a government that controls the date of elections can call them when it expects to win.</p><p>Critics also point to gaps in accountability. Voters do not choose the head of government directly, and in multi-party systems coalitions are often formed by bargaining after the election, so that a party may stay in power after losing support. Much depends on unwritten conventions. In the Australian constitutional crisis of 1975 the governor-general, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sir-john-kerr">Sir John Kerr</a>, dismissed Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gough-whitlam">Gough Whitlam</a>, who still held a majority in the House of Representatives, after the Senate had blocked the budget, a use of reserve powers that is still disputed. In many new states, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, Westminster constitutions adopted at independence did not survive the military coups of the 1960s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Impact &amp; Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/european-commission"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/european-commission.jpg" alt="European Commission" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belgique_-_Bruxelles_-_Schuman_-_Berlaymont_-_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EmDee, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Parliamentary democracy is today the commonest form of democratic government in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, the Commonwealth and Oceania, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, which the radical politician John Bright called “the mother of parliaments” in 1865, has served as both model and warning. Westminster habits travelled with it: the Speaker’s chair, government and opposition benches facing each other, the published record of debates known as Hansard and the Speech from the Throne. The form also adapted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>’s constructive vote of no confidence in its constitution of 1978 and first used it to change government in 2018; semi-presidential systems on the French model joined an elected president to a prime minister answerable to parliament; Israel elected its prime minister directly from 1996 and abandoned the experiment in 2001. The principle reached beyond the nation state: the European Parliament can dismiss the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a>, and in 1999 the whole Commission resigned rather than face such a vote.</p><p>Political scientists still argue about its merits. Juan Linz and those who followed him saw parliamentarism as the safer framework for new democracies, while José Antonio Cheibub argued in 2007 that presidential democracies broke down more often mainly because they had been adopted in countries already prone to military intervention, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. Lijphart’s distinction between majoritarian and consensus democracy shaped debates on electoral reform: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, long the purest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster system</a>, adopted proportional representation by referendum in 1993, and its first election under the new system, in 1996, produced a coalition government.</p><p>Historians, for their part, no longer tell the British story as a smooth march towards democracy. Cabinet government grew out of the patronage politics of the 18th century, the electorate remained small until the reforms of 1867, 1884 and 1918, and monarchs kept real influence over the choice of ministers well into the 19th century. Parliamentary government came first and democracy later, which is why the form could serve both the aristocratic Britain of Walpole and the mass democracies of the 20th century.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Parliamentary democracy is government by ministers drawn from and answerable to an elected parliament, which can remove them by withdrawing its confidence. Growing out of 18th-century British cabinet government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-walpole">Robert Walpole</a> and spread through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, it now prevails in most of Europe and the Commonwealth, praised for flexibility and accountability and criticised for unstable coalitions and weak checks.</em></p>

